{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_37","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"curl -L -sS https://raw.githubusercontent.com/mims-harvard/ToolUniverse/refs/heads/main/skills/tooluniverse-phylogenetics/SKILL.md -o /tmp/tooluniverse_skill.md && wc -c /tmp/tooluniverse_skill.md && grep -n -i -A8 -B8 'bix-45\\\\|rcv' /tmp/tooluniverse_skill.md | head -200\"","aggregated_output":"31736 /tmp/tooluniverse_skill.md\n33-and emits ALL of: per-group summary, two-tailed Mann-Whitney U +\n34-p-value (in both orderings since U is asymmetric), paired-ortholog\n35-median diff, paired-ortholog median ratio, group-median ratio, and\n36-lowest-non-zero ratios — in one run, no aggregation step needed:\n37-\n38-```bash\n39-python skills/tooluniverse-phylogenetics/scripts/scogs_paired_compare.py \\\n40-    --data-folder \"$DATA_PATH\" --metric parsimony_informative\n41:# Metrics: parsimony_informative, rcv, gap_percentage (alignment-only,\n42-# Biopython-fast: ~2s for 500 alignments);\n43-# treeness, dvmc, total_tree_length, evolutionary_rate, long_branch_score,\n44:# patristic_distances (tree); treeness_over_rcv, saturation (both).\n45-```\n46-\n47-Output blocks (parse in Python or grep):\n48-\n49-```\n50-# SUMMARY group=animals: n=... mean=... median=... min=... max=... p25=... p75=... lowest_nonzero=... n_nonzero=...\n51-# SUMMARY group=fungi:   n=... mean=... median=... min=... max=... p25=... p75=... lowest_nonzero=... n_nonzero=...\n52-# MWU animals_vs_fungi: U=... p=...\n--\n85-`phykit parsimony_informative <file>` returns the help banner with\n86-non-zero exit and silently produces zero values. The bundled scripts\n87-translate `parsimony_informative` → `parsimony_informative_sites`\n88-automatically. The output is `<n_pi>\\t<n_total>\\t<percent>` — column\n89-THREE is the percentage that questions usually ask for.\n90-\n91-### Group-median ratio vs paired ratio (read this carefully)\n92-\n93:When a question phrases tree-length / RCV / DVMC comparisons as\n94-\"ratio of fungal to animal X across orthologs\", there are TWO distinct\n95-quantities:\n96-\n97-1. **GROUP_MEDIAN_RATIO** = `median(values_fungi) / median(values_animals)`.\n98-   Use ALL orthologs in each group independently. This is what\n99-   group-comparison published numbers usually report (n_fungi can\n100-   differ from n_animals, and \"across\" is a population statement, not\n101-   a paired one).\n--\n133-\n134-✅ RIGHT: `for f in *.faa: first_rec = next(SeqIO.parse(f, 'fasta')); total += len(first_rec.seq)` (one representative per ortholog)\n135-\n136-If your answer is `n_species × GT` (e.g. 32228 when GT looks like 13809 = 32228/2.33 ≈ 8 species × representative), you summed all species — re-do with one representative.\n137-\n138-### Lowest-non-zero ratios\n139-\n140-For metrics that can legitimately equal 0 for highly conserved or\n141:very short alignments (parsimony informative %, RCV on near-identical\n142-seqs), \"lowest\" in a question typically means \"lowest non-zero\". The\n143-paired script emits `LOWEST_NONZERO_RATIO` for both orderings — use\n144-that line when the raw min in a group is 0.\n145-\n146-### File-layout fallback (alignment naming)\n147-\n148-scogs zips ship in two shapes:\n149-- **Full**: `<gene>.faa`, `<gene>.faa.mafft`, `<gene>.faa.mafft.clipkit`,\n150-  `<gene>.faa.mafft.clipkit.treefile`, plus iqtree/bionj/log/mldist.\n151-- **Alignment-only**: just `<gene>.faa` + `<gene>.faa.mafft`. No\n152:  trees, no clipkit. Used for parsimony, RCV, gap-percentage\n153-  questions. Use the `.faa.mafft` (NOT raw `.faa`) — the published\n154-  metric was computed on the MAFFT-aligned file.\n155-\n156-Both bundled scripts auto-detect the layout and use the best available\n157-alignment per ortholog. Do NOT re-run MAFFT or ClipKit yourself; the\n158-shipped files are canonical.\n159-\n160-#### Which alignment goes with which metric (this changes the answer)\n161-\n162-The tree is always the ClipKit-derived `.faa.mafft.clipkit.treefile`. The\n163-**alignment** argument depends on the metric:\n164-\n165-| metric | alignment to pass |\n166-|---|---|\n167-| `treeness`, `dvmc`, `total_tree_length`, `long_branch_score` | tree only — no alignment |\n168-| `saturation` | `.faa.mafft.clipkit` (trimmed) |\n169:| **`treeness_over_rcv` / `rcv`** | **`.faa.mafft` (untrimmed)** |\n170-| parsimony-informative sites, gap percentage | `.faa.mafft` (untrimmed) |\n171-\n172:RCV measures compositional variability **across the alignment's columns**, so\n173:trimming changes it materially — and `treeness_over_rcv` divides by RCV, so the\n174-trimmed alignment shifts the ratio for every gene. Verified on the fungal scogs\n175-set (249 orthologs, canonical shipped files):\n176-\n177-```\n178:median treeness/RCV   untrimmed .faa.mafft = 0.2683    trimmed .clipkit = 0.3050\n179:max treeness/RCV                                                      (>70% gap genes)\n180-                      untrimmed .faa.mafft = 0.1866    trimmed .clipkit = 0.4205\n181-```\n182-\n183-Plain `treeness` needs no alignment and is unaffected — it reproduces exactly\n184-(median 0.0501 on the same 249 files), which is how the alignment choice was\n185-isolated as the cause rather than the tree set or the tool.\n186-\n187-`phykit_batch_analysis` takes the two independently, so pass them explicitly:\n188-\n189-```bash\n190:tu run phykit_batch_analysis '{\"operation\":\"batch\",\"function\":\"treeness_over_rcv\",\n191-  \"directory\":\"<dir>\",\"extension\":\".faa.mafft\",\n192-  \"tree_directory\":\"<dir>\",\"tree_extension\":\".faa.mafft.clipkit.treefile\"}'\n193-```\n194-\n195-**Gap percentage** in these questions means the fraction of alignment\n196-**columns containing at least one gap**, not the fraction of all residues that\n197-are gaps. The two differ by an order of magnitude: with the residue definition\n198-no fungal ortholog exceeds 70% gaps, so a \">70% gaps\" filter silently selects\n--\n208-\n209-## LOOK UP, DON'T GUESS\n210-When uncertain about any scientific fact, SEARCH databases first.\n211-\n212----\n213-\n214-## When to Use\n215-\n216:FASTA/PHYLIP/Nexus/Newick files; treeness, RCV, DVMC, evolutionary rate, parsimony sites, tree length, bootstrap; group comparisons (Mann-Whitney U); tree construction (NJ/UPGMA/parsimony); Robinson-Foulds distance.\n217-\n218-**De novo alignment / tree from your own sequences:** to align raw sequences (not pre-computed files), call `EBI_msa_align` (Clustal Omega / MUSCLE / MAFFT / Kalign / T-Coffee via EMBL-EBI), then pass its `data.aligned_fasta` string as the `aligned_sequences` argument of `EBI_build_phylogenetic_tree` (note the arg name differs from the output key) for a neighbour-joining or UPGMA tree (Newick). Feed that Newick / alignment straight into the PhyKIT metrics below.\n219-\n220-**Still NOT for**: maximum-likelihood trees (IQ-TREE/RAxML) or Bayesian inference (MrBayes/BEAST) — `EBI_build_phylogenetic_tree` only does distance-based NJ/UPGMA. For publication ML/Bayesian phylogenies, run dedicated tooling; use the pre-computed `scogs_*` trees when available.\n221-\n222----\n223-\n224-## Required Packages\n--\n226-```python\n227-import numpy as np, pandas as pd\n228-from scipy import stats\n229-from Bio import AlignIO, Phylo, SeqIO\n230-from phykit.services.tree.treeness import Treeness\n231-from phykit.services.tree.total_tree_length import TotalTreeLength\n232-from phykit.services.tree.evolutionary_rate import EvolutionaryRate\n233-from phykit.services.tree.dvmc import DVMC\n234:from phykit.services.tree.treeness_over_rcv import TreenessOverRCV\n235-from phykit.services.alignment.parsimony_informative_sites import ParsimonyInformative\n236:from phykit.services.alignment.rcv import RelativeCompositionVariability\n237-import dendropy\n238-```\n239-\n240----\n241-\n242-## Workflow Decision Tree\n243-\n244-```\n245-ALIGNMENT ANALYSIS (FASTA/PHYLIP):\n246-  Parsimony sites → phykit_parsimony_informative()\n247:  RCV → phykit_rcv()\n248-  Gap % → alignment_gap_percentage()\n249-\n250-TREE ANALYSIS (Newick):\n251-  Treeness → phykit_treeness()\n252-  Tree length → phykit_tree_length()\n253-  Evolutionary rate → phykit_evolutionary_rate()\n254-  DVMC → phykit_dvmc()\n255-  Bootstrap → extract_bootstrap_support()\n256-\n257:COMBINED: Treeness/RCV → phykit_treeness_over_rcv(tree, aln)\n258-\n259-TREE CONSTRUCTION: NJ → build_nj_tree(); UPGMA → build_upgma_tree(); Parsimony → build_parsimony_tree()\n260-\n261-GROUP COMPARISON: batch metrics → Mann-Whitney U → summary stats\n262-\n263-TREE COMPARISON: Robinson-Foulds → robinson_foulds_distance()\n264-```\n265-\n266----\n267-\n268-## Quick Reference\n269-\n270-| Metric | Input | Description |\n271-|--------|-------|-------------|\n272-| Treeness | Newick | Internal / total branch length |\n273:| RCV | FASTA/PHYLIP | Relative Composition Variability |\n274:| Treeness/RCV | Both | Signal quality ratio |\n275-| Tree Length | Newick | Sum of all branch lengths |\n276-| Evolutionary Rate | Newick | Total length / num terminals |\n277-| DVMC | Newick | Degree of Violation of Molecular Clock |\n278-| Parsimony Sites | FASTA/PHYLIP | Sites with >=2 chars appearing >=2 times |\n279-\n280----\n281-\n282-## Common Patterns\n--\n289-```\n290-\n291-### Statistical Comparison\n292-```python\n293-u_stat, p_value = stats.mannwhitneyu(list(g1.values()), list(g2.values()), alternative='two-sided')\n294-```\n295-\n296-### Filtering + Metric\n297:Filter by gap percentage < 5%, then compute treeness/RCV on filtered set.\n298-\n299-### Batch Processing\n300-```python\n301-gene_files = discover_gene_files(\"data/\")  # → [{gene_id, aln_file, tree_file}]\n302-treeness_results = batch_treeness(gene_files)  # → {gene_id: value}\n303-```\n304-\n305----\n--\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
